export const profiles = {
  "breeds/small-animals/sugar-glider.html": {
    description: "Sugar gliders weigh about 4 to 5 ounces and can live 12 to 15 years. They are nocturnal, colonial, and illegal to keep in some states. A single glider in a small cage is a welfare problem.",
    faqTitle: "Sugar glider questions",
    faqs: [
      { q: "Can I keep one sugar glider?", a: "A lone glider needs hours of direct contact every night or it self-mutilates. Two or more compatible gliders are the usual plan. One glider plus a busy human who is asleep at night is not a colony." },
      { q: "Are they legal?", a: "Not in every state. California, Hawaii, and Alaska restrict or ban them, and some cities do too. Check before you buy. A breeder who ships does not write your state law." },
      { q: "Are they flying squirrels?", a: "No. Sugar gliders are marsupials, Petaurus breviceps. North American flying squirrels are a different animal. This page is the glider." },
    ],
    html: `<h2>A night animal that glides, and screams</h2>
<p>Sugar gliders weigh about 4 to 5 ounces. Captive life is often 12 to 15 years, which is a long commitment for an animal that is awake when you want to sleep. They need a tall cage, well beyond a hamster tub, with branches and a pouch. The diet has to be built for calcium. A fruit-only menu produces hind-limb paralysis from metabolic bone disease.</p>
<p>They bond to their group. Hands are a slow project, not a day-one cuddle. The smell is real. The barking at night is real. Apartment neighbors may not find it charming.</p>
<h2>Calcium, law, and a vet who has seen one</h2>
<p>A glider that drags a back leg, or that stops eating the prepared diet and only licks fruit, needs a diet overhaul and often a vet. Ordinary cat-and-dog clinics may decline them. <a href="https://www.aspca.org/pet-care">ASPCA pet care notes</a> are a start for small mammals, not a glider recipe. Appetite loss is the <a href="/vet/symptoms/small-mammal-not-eating">small-mammal not-eating page</a>.</p>`,
  },

  "breeds/small-animals/gerbil.html": {
    description: "Gerbils weigh a few ounces and often live 3 to 5 years. They want a same-sex pair, deep bedding to tunnel in, and they are illegal to keep in California.",
    faqTitle: "Gerbil questions",
    faqs: [
      { q: "Can gerbils live alone?", a: "They can, and many are miserable that way. A same-sex pair from a young age is the usual plan. Adult strangers often fight. Do not add a new gerbil to a grieving one without a careful split cage." },
      { q: "Why are they illegal in some places?", a: "California bans gerbils as pets. Other states do not. Check your state before you treat a pet-store tank as permission." },
      { q: "What is a degloved tail?", a: "The skin can pull off a gerbil tail if you grab it. Never pick a gerbil up by the tail. The injury often means amputating what is left." },
    ],
    html: `<h2>Desert rats that need a tunnel</h2>
<p>Gerbils weigh about 2 to 4 ounces and often live 3 to 5 years. They are awake in bursts through the day and evening. A 10-gallon tank with a inch of bedding is not enough. They want deep bedding to dig, a wheel, and a lid, because they chew plastic and they kick bedding out of short cages.</p>
<p>Pairs do better than singles. Watch for a gerbil that is barbered bald or sleeping apart and bleeding. That pair is over. Seizures happen in some lines and look terrifying. A one-off seizure still deserves a note to a vet. A gerbil that is seizing and not recovering needs help the same day.</p>
<h2>Scent glands, teeth, and the California rule</h2>
<p>The orange scent gland on the belly is normal. A sore that grows is not. Teeth overgrow if the gerbil cannot chew. A fat face can be an impacted cheek pouch. <a href="https://www.aspca.org/pet-care">ASPCA</a> covers small-mammal basics. A gerbil that ignores food is small enough to crash quickly, which is why the <a href="/vet/symptoms/small-mammal-not-eating">not-eating page</a> matters.</p>`,
  },

  "breeds/small-animals/fancy-rat.html": {
    description: "Fancy rats weigh about half a pound to a pound and a half and often live only 2 to 3 years. They need a companion, and respiratory disease and mammary tumors are the common endings.",
    faqTitle: "Fancy rat questions",
    faqs: [
      { q: "Do rats need a friend?", a: "Yes. A lone rat is a depressed rat. Keep same-sex pairs or a neutered group. Introducing adults takes a neutral space, not a shove into the resident's cage." },
      { q: "Why do they honk and sneeze?", a: "Mycoplasma is nearly universal. Some rats stay mildly snuffly. A rat that gasps, loses weight, or has a porphyrin-stained nose and eye that is worse than usual needs a vet who will use rat-safe antibiotics. Pet-store drops are not a plan." },
      { q: "How long do they really live?", a: "Two to three years is honest. A rat that reaches four is old. Plan the goodbye when you buy the baby." },
    ],
    html: `<h2>Short lives, obvious personalities</h2>
<p>Fancy rats weigh about 0.5 to 1.5 pounds and live about 2 to 3 years. They are social, trainable, and cleaner than their reputation if the cage is. A wire floor cuts feet. A tank with no airflow grows ammonia and wrecks lungs. Give space, hammocks, and a litter corner they will mostly use.</p>
<p>Females develop mammary tumors often enough that a new lump should be seen, not watched for a month. Males can be a bit lazier and smell stronger. Both sexes need a friend. Handling from a young age makes a shoulder rat. Grabbing a sleeping rat makes a biter.</p>
<h2>Lungs first</h2>
<p>Respiratory disease is the illness you will meet. Red tears are porphyrin and can mean stress or sickness, not blood. A rat that breathes with its mouth open is in trouble now. <a href="https://www.merckvetmanual.com/">Merck</a> covers murine respiratory disease in more detail than a pet page should pretend to. Skipping meals is the <a href="/vet/symptoms/small-mammal-not-eating">small-mammal appetite page</a>.</p>`,
  },

  "breeds/small-animals/fancy-mouse.html": {
    description: "Fancy mice weigh under an ounce and often live 1.5 to 3 years. Females can share a cage. Adult males usually cannot. The life is short, and the smell is the male's calling card.",
    faqTitle: "Fancy mouse questions",
    faqs: [
      { q: "Can two male mice live together?", a: "Adult males fight. House a male alone or with females only if you are prepared for a population explosion. Female groups, introduced young, are the social cage." },
      { q: "Why does the cage smell?", a: "Males mark. Clean the urine spots often and do not use a strong pine or cedar bedding. Ammonia, not the mouse, is what damages the airway." },
      { q: "Are they good kids' pets?", a: "They are fast, fragile, and nocturnal. A child can help feed a mouse. A child should not be the only keeper, and a mouse should not be a pocket pet that gets squeezed." },
    ],
    html: `<h2>An ounce, and a short calendar</h2>
<p>Fancy mice weigh about half an ounce to an ounce and live roughly 1.5 to 3 years. They are busy at night and quick enough to vanish behind a dresser during a cage clean. Floor space matters more than a tall tube system that nobody can clean. A wheel with a solid surface saves tails.</p>
<p>Feed a pellet made for mice, plus a little fresh food, and skip the sticky seed-bar that is mostly honey. They are escape artists. A lid that "usually" stays on will be the night you lose one in the kitchen.</p>
<h2>Tumors and breathing</h2>
<p>Mammary tumors and respiratory infections are the common end. A mouse is so small that a day without food is already late. Weigh it if you can. A dropping scale is the early sign. <a href="https://www.aspca.org/pet-care">ASPCA small-pet notes</a> are general. The <a href="/vet/symptoms/small-mammal-not-eating">not-eating page</a> is the urgent version.</p>`,
  },

  "breeds/small-animals/degu.html": {
    description: "Degus weigh about 6 to 10 ounces and often live 6 to 9 years. They are social, they get diabetes if you feed them sugar, and they need a dust bath and something to chew.",
    faqTitle: "Degu questions",
    faqs: [
      { q: "Can a degu eat fruit?", a: "Almost none. Sugar is how degus become diabetic and develop cataracts. Hay, a measured pellet made for them, and the occasional plain vegetable are the diet. A yogurt drop is not a treat. It is a problem." },
      { q: "Do they need company?", a: "Yes. Keep a same-sex pair or a group that was raised together. A single degu is a welfare failure unless a vet has a reason." },
      { q: "What is the dust bath for?", a: "Coat health, a few times a week, not a pool they live in. Take the dust out after they bathe so it does not become a litter box." },
    ],
    html: `<h2>A Chilean rodent that should not see a raisin</h2>
<p>Degus weigh about 6 to 10 ounces and often live 6 to 9 years. They are diurnal enough to be awake when you are, which is rarer than hamster marketing suggests. They chew. A plastic cage is a snack. Multi-level space, hay, and safe wood are the housing. The tail skin can slip off if you grab it. Scoop them up instead.</p>
<p>They bark and whistle. They are not a quiet child's first pet, and they are not a hamster with a longer life. By the time a degu's lens turns cloudy, the sugary food that caused it has already been eaten. Fruit and sweet treats do not belong in the cage.</p>
<h2>Teeth and blood sugar</h2>
<p>Incisors and cheek teeth overgrow when the diet is soft. A degu that drools or drops food needs a dental exam under anesthesia by someone who treats exotic mammals. Diabetes shows up as drinking, weight loss, and cloudy eyes. <a href="https://www.merckvetmanual.com/">Merck</a> is the clinical reference. A degu off its hay is the <a href="/vet/symptoms/small-mammal-not-eating">appetite page</a>.</p>`,
  },

  "breeds/small-animals/roborovski-hamster.html": {
    description: "Roborovski hamsters are about 2 inches long and can live around 3 years, which is long for a hamster. They are the fastest pet hamster and a poor choice if you want to hold something.",
    faqTitle: "Roborovski hamster questions",
    faqs: [
      { q: "Can I tame a Roborovski?", a: "You can get one to take food from your hand. Many never enjoy being held. If the dream is a pocket hamster, choose a Syrian and still expect a nocturnal animal." },
      { q: "Can they live together?", a: "Same-sex pairs or small groups sometimes work if they grew up together and the cage is large. Fights still happen. A wounded hamster has to be separated the night you notice blood." },
      { q: "How much space?", a: "At least 450 square inches of floor, and more is better. A colorful tube kit from a toy aisle is not that. They run a long way on a solid wheel." },
    ],
    html: `<h2>The tiny one that vanishes</h2>
<p>Roborovski hamsters, Phodopus roborovskii, are about 1.5 to 2 inches and the longest-lived of the common pet hamsters when things go well, sometimes past 3 years. They are desert animals from Mongolia and China. They bolt. A hand that cups too slowly is a hand they launch from. Watch them in a big enclosure. That is the fun.</p>
<p>They are nocturnal. A child who wants to play at 4 p.m. will be disturbing a sleeping animal. Deep bedding for burrows matters more than a lookout loft. They do not need a friend the way a rat does, and a badly matched pair is a fight you will hear at midnight.</p>
<h2>Wet tail is not a brand name</h2>
<p>Diarrhea in a hamster is an emergency, whatever the pet store called it. Teeth still overgrow. A hamster that looks "fine" and is lighter in the hand is not fine. <a href="https://www.aspca.org/pet-care">ASPCA</a> small-mammal pages are general. The <a href="/vet/symptoms/small-mammal-not-eating">not-eating page</a> fits a Robo that ignores its food for a night, because they are too small to wait out.</p>`,
  },

  "breeds/small-animals/winter-white.html": {
    description: "Winter white dwarf hamsters weigh about 1 to 2 ounces and often live 1.5 to 2 years. They can change coat with the season. Fights are common enough that many keepers house them alone.",
    faqTitle: "Winter white hamster questions",
    faqs: [
      { q: "Do they turn white?", a: "In short daylight some do, from gray to white. A warm, brightly lit room may keep the summer coat. A coat change is not illness. A bald patch is." },
      { q: "Can they live with a Campbell's hamster?", a: "Hybrids happen and they are not a project to start. House winter whites with winter whites if you house them together at all, and be ready to separate them." },
      { q: "Are they good for kids?", a: "They are small, fast, and asleep when school gets out. They bite when grabbed. A child can help clean a cage. The child should not be the handler who picks the hamster up for fun." },
    ],
    html: `<h2>A dwarf that might go white</h2>
<p>Winter white hamsters, Phodopus sungorus, weigh about 1 to 2 ounces and live roughly 1.5 to 2 years. They are nocturnal and quick. The cage needs floor space, a solid wheel, and bedding deep enough to burrow. Tubes that are too narrow are a stuck-hamster story.</p>
<p>People argue about pairs. The safe advice is that a single winter white lives a normal life, and a pair only works with space, a shared upbringing, and a second cage ready. Blood on the bedding ends the experiment. They are not Syrians, and they are not Roborovskis. Name the species when you buy one.</p>
<h2>Diabetes stories and real diarrhea</h2>
<p>Winter whites are less associated with the diabetes talk that follows Campbell's hamsters, and they still get obese on sunflower seed. A seed mix as the whole diet is how cheek pouches fill and bodies fail. Diarrhea is urgent. <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet">VCA</a> has small-mammal feeding notes worth reading. A night of uneaten food is the <a href="/vet/symptoms/small-mammal-not-eating">appetite page</a>.</p>`,
  },

  "breeds/small-animals/dwarf-campbell.html": {
    description: "Campbell's dwarf hamsters weigh about 1 to 2 ounces and often live 1.5 to 2 years. They are nippy, nocturnal, and often kept alone because pairs fight.",
    faqTitle: "Campbell's dwarf hamster questions",
    faqs: [
      { q: "Are Campbell's hamsters social?", a: "They can live with their own kind and they often should not. Fights are sudden. A solitary Campbell's in a large cage is a fair life. A scarred pair is not a success." },
      { q: "Do they get diabetes?", a: "The species is prone to it, especially on sweet or fatty seed. A hamster that drinks constantly and loses weight needs a vet. Take the yogurt chips out of the diet tonight." },
      { q: "How do they differ from winter whites?", a: "Campbell's are stockier, often with a dorsal stripe, and they do not turn white in winter. Pet shops mix the names and sell hybrids. Ask, and expect a guess." },
    ],
    html: `<h2>Small, striped, and not a finger pet</h2>
<p>Campbell's dwarf hamsters weigh about 1 to 2 ounces and live about 1.5 to 2 years. They are nocturnal. A cage of at least 450 square inches of floor is the start, with a wheel that does not bend the back. They bite when startled. That is ordinary, not a mean individual you can scold.</p>
<p>House them alone unless you already know how you will separate a fight. Hybrids with winter whites are common in pet shops and are not a hardier pet. They are a muddle. Feed a pellet, a little fresh food, and almost no sugary treats.</p>
<h2>Thirst, teeth, and a wet tail end</h2>
<p>Constant drinking can be diabetes. Diarrhea dehydrates a one-ounce animal in hours. Overgrown teeth make a hamster pouch food and starve. <a href="https://www.aspca.org/pet-care">ASPCA</a> small-pet pages are the overview. A Campbell's that looks hunched and cold needs warmth on the way to a clinic, and the <a href="/vet/symptoms/small-mammal-not-eating">not-eating page</a> if the food is still full.</p>`,
  },

  "breeds/small-animals/lionhead.html": {
    description: "Lionhead rabbits weigh about 2.5 to 3.75 pounds and often live 7 to 10 years. The mane is wool. It mats, and a rabbit that stops eating is a same-day emergency.",
    faqTitle: "Lionhead rabbit questions",
    faqs: [
      { q: "How often do you groom a lionhead?", a: "Several times a week, and daily when the mane is shedding. Mats pull on skin. A heavily matted rabbit may need a vet to clip the coat, because rabbit skin tears under household clippers." },
      { q: "Can a lionhead live alone?", a: "Rabbits are social. A bonded pair is the better life if you can neuter and introduce them properly. A single rabbit with a person who actually sits on the floor can work. A single rabbit in a hutch cannot." },
      { q: "What is GI stasis?", a: "The gut slows and the rabbit stops eating and producing droppings. It is painful and it kills. Syringe-feeding and a vet the same day are the response, not a wait to see if the rabbit 'perks up.'" },
    ],
    html: `<h2>A small rabbit with a wool wig</h2>
<p>Lionheads weigh about 2.5 to 3.75 pounds and often live 7 to 10 years. Hay is the diet. Pellets are a measured handful. Vegetables are a side. Fruit is a rare treat. The mane does not change that. A rabbit fed mostly pellets and carrots is a dental and a stasis case in progress.</p>
<p>They need room to hop, a litter box, and a floor that is not wire. Cages sold as "rabbit starter kits" are usually a crate you would not leave a cat in. Exercise for several hours a day is the housing plan, or a pen that is already that large.</p>
<h2>Teeth, guts, and a mat</h2>
<p>Cheek teeth overgrow and the rabbit drools. The gut stops when pain or a bad diet slows it. No droppings and no interest in hay is an emergency the same day. <a href="https://www.aspca.org/pet-care">ASPCA</a> rabbit notes are worth reading before the first missed meal. The <a href="/vet/symptoms/small-mammal-not-eating">not-eating page</a> applies the hour you notice.</p>`,
  },

  "breeds/small-animals/mini-lop.html": {
    description: "Mini lop rabbits weigh about 4.5 to 6.5 pounds and can live 7 to 14 years. Lop ears are not a personality. They are a site for infection, and the teeth still need hay.",
    faqTitle: "Mini lop questions",
    faqs: [
      { q: "Why do lop ears get infected?", a: "They fold and they stay damp. A smell, a head shake, or a rabbit that resents a touch on the ear needs a vet. Do not pour vinegar or leftover dog ear cleaner down a lop ear." },
      { q: "Are they calmer than other rabbits?", a: "Some are. The ear shape does not guarantee a lap rabbit. Sit on the floor and let the rabbit choose. Picking one up whenever you want a cuddle makes a kicker." },
      { q: "How big is the enclosure?", a: "A pen of at least 4 by 2 feet is a start, plus hours out of it. A hutch in the yard is not a life, and outdoor rabbits meet heat, flies, and predators." },
    ],
    html: `<h2>Floppy ears, ordinary rabbit rules</h2>
<p>Mini lops weigh about 4.5 to 6.5 pounds. A well-kept one can live anywhere from 7 years into the teens. Hay all day, a handful of pellets, and greens are the menu. They are social. A bonded, neutered pair is easier than a lonely rabbit that chews the baseboard because nobody came home.</p>
<p>The lop is cute and it hides ear disease. Check under the flap. Teeth still meet badly in this breed, the way they do in many small rabbits. A wet chin is dental pain until a vet says otherwise.</p>
<h2>Stasis does not wait for Monday</h2>
<p>A mini lop that hunches, grinds its teeth, or leaves the hay untouched needs pain control and a clinic, not a new toy. Droppings that shrink to nothing are the same emergency. <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet">VCA</a> explains gastrointestinal stasis. Use the <a href="/vet/symptoms/small-mammal-not-eating">appetite page</a> on the way.</p>`,
  },

  "breeds/small-animals/netherland-dwarf.html": {
    description: "Netherland dwarf rabbits weigh about 1 to 2.5 pounds and often live 10 to 12 years. They are not a child's stuffed animal. They are a tiny rabbit with dental problems and a long life if the diet is hay.",
    faqTitle: "Netherland dwarf questions",
    faqs: [
      { q: "Are Netherland dwarfs good for kids?", a: "Usually no. They are fragile, quick, and often nippy. An adult who wants a small rabbit can do well. A child who wants to carry a bunny around will break one or get bitten." },
      { q: "Why are their faces so short?", a: "The dwarf face crowds the teeth. Malocclusion is common. A rabbit that drops pellets or has a wet chin needs the molars and the incisors examined." },
      { q: "How long do they live?", a: "Often 10 to 12 years. That is longer than many families expect from a rabbit that fits in two hands." },
    ],
    html: `<h2>Two pounds and a decade</h2>
<p>Netherland dwarfs weigh about 1.1 to 2.5 pounds and often live 10 to 12 years. They are energetic and not especially interested in being held. Hay is most of the diet. A seed-and-carrot habit will grow the wrong teeth and stop the gut. They need space to run that looks absurd next to their size, because a small animal still has legs.</p>
<p>Neuter and spay. Uterine cancer is common in female rabbits that are left intact, and intact males spray and fight. A pair can work after surgery and a proper introduction. A pair of strangers dumped together is a fight.</p>
<h2>A short face is a dental plan</h2>
<p>Incisors that curl and molars that spur are the breed tax. Some need repeated dentals for life. Budget that before you fall for the face. GI stasis is the other emergency, same as in any rabbit. <a href="https://www.aspca.org/pet-care">ASPCA</a> covers rabbit feeding in plain language. A dwarf that will not eat hay by evening is the <a href="/vet/symptoms/small-mammal-not-eating">not-eating page</a>.</p>`,
  },

  "breeds/small-animals/prairie-dog.html": {
    description: "Prairie dogs weigh about 1 to 3 pounds and can live 8 to 12 years. They are colonial, destructive, and restricted in some states. This is not a starter pet.",
    faqTitle: "Prairie dog questions",
    faqs: [
      { q: "Are prairie dogs legal?", a: "Not everywhere. Some states ban them or require permits, partly because of plague and other diseases. Check state and city rules before you believe a seller." },
      { q: "Can they be handled?", a: "Some captive-bred animals tolerate their own person. They bite, they are not domesticated dogs, and a wild-caught prairie dog is a bad idea and often an illegal one." },
      { q: "Do they need company?", a: "They are highly social. A single prairie dog in a cage is a poor life. Most private homes cannot provide a group and the space they use to dig." },
    ],
    html: `<h2>A burrowing squirrel, not a cartoon</h2>
<p>Prairie dogs weigh about 1 to 3 pounds and can live 8 to 12 years if they are not wild-caught and stressed into an early death. They dig, they chew, they bark, and they need a large enclosure or a room you are willing to surrender. Hay and grasses are the diet. Seed mixes and junk food make fat, sick animals.</p>
<p>They can carry fleas and, in wild populations, plague. That is a reason for a vet who understands the risk, not a reason to panic at a captive-bred animal and also not a reason to treat one like a hamster from a fair. Most people should not keep them.</p>
<h2>Teeth, heat, and a law book</h2>
<p>Dental disease and obesity are the captive problems. A prairie dog that stops eating needs an exotic vet quickly. Do not release one outdoors. A pet prairie dog is not a native restoration project. <a href="https://www.aspca.org/pet-care">ASPCA</a> guidance on exotic pets is the cautious starting point. Appetite loss is the <a href="/vet/symptoms/small-mammal-not-eating">small-mammal page</a>.</p>`,
  },

  "breeds/small-animals/hermit-crab-pet.html": {
    description: "Land hermit crabs can live for decades and usually do not. They need heat, humidity, salt water and fresh water, and a shell to move into. A dry painted-shell crab is a dying crab.",
    faqTitle: "Hermit crab questions",
    faqs: [
      { q: "How long do hermit crabs live?", a: "The long end is 10 years and more, sometimes decades. Most pet crabs die in months from a dry tank, tap water, or a shell glued shut. The lifespan is a husbandry score." },
      { q: "Do they need friends?", a: "They are social. A group in a large tank does better than one crab in a souvenir cage. They also fight over shells. Extra empty shells in several sizes are equipment, not clutter." },
      { q: "What if my crab buries itself?", a: "It may be molting. Do not dig it up. A molt is when they are soft and easily killed by a helpful owner. Isolate a molting crab only if the others are chewing on it." },
    ],
    html: `<h2>A decade, if the tank is actually tropical</h2>
<p>Land hermit crabs are often sold as a few dollars of beach souvenir. The honest setup is a tank with deep substrate for molting, heat, humidity high enough that the gills work, a pool of dechlorinated fresh water, and a pool of marine salt water. Both pools. They are not fish and they are not a desert animal that lives on a dry sponge.</p>
<p>Painted shells can be toxic and too small. Offer natural shells. Food is a mix of plants, protein, and calcium, not a single pellet forever. They need darkness and quiet while they molt, which can take weeks.</p>
<h2>Molts, shells, and a dead crab that looked asleep</h2>
<p>A crab out of its shell is in crisis. A crab that smells of decay is already dead. Do not rinse them in tap water for fun. <a href="https://www.aspca.org/pet-care">ASPCA</a> exotic-pet cautions apply. There is no honest version of this animal as a pocket pet for a weekend.</p>`,
  },

  "breeds/small-animals/silkie-guinea-pig.html": {
    description: "Silkie guinea pigs weigh about 1.5 to 2.5 pounds and often live 5 to 7 years. The long hair is the work. Hay, vitamin C, and a second pig are the rest.",
    faqTitle: "Silkie guinea pig questions",
    faqs: [
      { q: "How often do you bathe a silkie?", a: "Rarely. Brush the coat daily or every other day so mats do not form against the skin. A bath is for a dirty rear end, then a full dry. A wet longhair pig in a cold room gets chilled." },
      { q: "Do they need vitamin C?", a: "Yes, every day, like every guinea pig. They cannot make their own. Pellets help and still go stale. A pig that is limping or has a rough coat may be scorbutic, which is a diet failure." },
      { q: "Can they live alone?", a: "They should not. Guinea pigs are a pair or a group. One silkie in a show coat is still a herd animal." },
    ],
    html: `<h2>A guinea pig in a wig</h2>
<p>Silkies, also called shelties in some clubs, weigh about 1.5 to 2.5 pounds and often live 5 to 7 years. Hair grows back over the body. It wraps around feet and picks up hay, urine, and pellets. Daily grooming is the breed. If you do not want that chore, choose a short-coated pig. The personality underneath is still a guinea pig: talkative, food-motivated, and unhappy alone.</p>
<p>Floor space of at least 7.5 square feet for one is a minimum, and a pair needs more. Hay is the main food. Vitamin C is not optional. Vitamin drops in the water bottle are a weak plan because the vitamin breaks down and the pig may not drink enough.</p>
<h2>Scurvy, stones, and a mat against the skin</h2>
<p>A limp, a sore joint, or a pig that screams when picked up can be vitamin C deficiency or a stone. Both need a vet who sees guinea pigs. Matted hair hides skin infections. <a href="https://vcahospitals.com/know-your-pet">VCA</a> covers guinea pig vitamin C. A pig that will not eat hay by the end of the day is the <a href="/vet/symptoms/small-mammal-not-eating">not-eating page</a>.</p>`,
  },

  "breeds/small-animals/skinny-pig.html": {
    description: "Skinny pigs are hairless guinea pigs, about 1.5 to 2.5 pounds, often living 5 to 7 years. They need warmth, skin care, vitamin C, and a companion. Hairless is not easier.",
    faqTitle: "Skinny pig questions",
    faqs: [
      { q: "Are skinny pigs hypoallergenic?", a: "They have less hair. People still react to dander and saliva. The skin needs more care, not less, so this is a poor shortcut for an allergy." },
      { q: "How warm should the room be?", a: "Comfortably warm for a person in a T-shirt, and free of drafts. A hairless pig in a cold basement shakes and gets sick. Fleece they can burrow into helps. A heat lamp that can burn is the wrong tool." },
      { q: "Do they still need vitamin C?", a: "Yes. Hairless does not change the biology. Scurvy looks like pain, a rough appetite, and a pig that does not want to move." },
    ],
    html: `<h2>A guinea pig without the coat</h2>
<p>Skinny pigs weigh about 1.5 to 2.5 pounds and often live 5 to 7 years. The hairlessness came out of a laboratory colony in the late 1970s. Some have a bit of hair on the face and feet. The skin is dry, oily, or spotty depending on the pig, and it can tear. They need a companion guinea pig, which can be a normal-coated friend, and they need hay and vitamin C like any other pig.</p>
<p>Bedding should not be a dusty wood product that irritates bare skin. Fleece liners that you wash are the usual answer. They eat more than a coated pig in a cold room because they spend calories staying warm. Keep the room warm so they are not eating their way out of a chill.</p>
<h2>Skin, cold, and the same emergency as any pig</h2>
<p>Scrapes, fungal patches, and crusts need a vet who will not prescribe a random dog ointment. A skinny pig that stops eating is as urgent as a furry one, and faster if the room is cool. <a href="https://www.aspca.org/pet-care">ASPCA</a> guinea pig care is the diet baseline. Use the <a href="/vet/symptoms/small-mammal-not-eating">not-eating page</a> the day the hay is ignored.</p>`,
  },

  "breeds/birds/conure.html": {
    description: "Conure is a group, not one bird. Sizes run about 9 to 20 inches and lives often 20 to 30 years. A green-cheek and a sun conure do not share a noise level or a cage.",
    faqTitle: "Conure questions",
    faqs: [
      { q: "Which conure is quiet?", a: "None are silent. Pyrrhura conures, such as green-cheeks, are usually quieter than Aratinga and sun conures. 'Quieter' still means a contact call through a wall." },
      { q: "Can I keep two species together?", a: "Do not assume it. Many conures are territorial, and a larger bird can maim a smaller one. One species, and often one bird, unless you know the pair." },
      { q: "How long do they live?", a: "Often 20 to 30 years, sometimes longer. A conure is not a five-year impulse buy that happens to be colorful." },
    ],
    html: `<h2>Name the conure or do not buy the cage</h2>
<p>Conure covers several genera from Central and South America. A green-cheek is about the size of a chubby cockatiel. A sun or a jenday is louder and often needier. Adults in the group run roughly 9 to 20 inches, and 20 to 30 years is a fair life if the bird is not kept on seed in a corner. Noise runs from moderate to genuinely neighborhood-level. Talking is limited. People who want a talking parrot and buy a conure for the colors get a screamer with a vocabulary of a few words.</p>
<p>Pellets, vegetables, and a little fruit are the diet. A cage of 24 inches square is a minimum that is already wrong for the larger species. Out-of-cage time is every day. They chew. They bite during a hormonal season. That is a parrot, not a betrayal.</p>
<h2>One bird, one avian vet</h2>
<p>Find a clinic through <a href="https://www.aav.org">AAV</a> before the first quiet day. A fluffed conure on the cage floor is the <a href="/vet/symptoms/bird-quiet">quiet bird page</a>. Sun conures and green-cheeks have their own pages because the details diverge.</p>`,
  },

  "breeds/birds/pionus.html": {
    description: "Pionus parrots are about 10 to 12 inches and often live 25 to 40 years. They are quieter than an Amazon, and a wheeze when they are nervous can be normal rather than a respiratory crisis.",
    faqTitle: "Pionus questions",
    faqs: [
      { q: "Why does my pionus sound stuffy?", a: "Many pionus make a snuffling sound when they are excited or alarmed. It can be normal. Labored breathing, a tail bob at rest, or a bird that is fluffed and quiet is not that sound. That bird needs a vet." },
      { q: "Are they good apartment birds?", a: "Better than a macaw or a sun conure. They still call. An apartment can work if you are home and the neighbors are not sharing a paper wall." },
      { q: "Which pionus is this?", a: "Eight species share the genus. Blue-headed, white-capped, bronze-winged, and dusky are not the same size or the same price. Ask for the species on the receipt." },
    ],
    html: `<h2>Calmer than an Amazon, and still a long-lived parrot</h2>
<p>Pionus are stocky parrots, about 10 to 12 inches and 200 to 280 grams, often living 25 to 40 years. They are calmer and quieter than the famous talking parrots, and they are still parrots. A cage of at least 24 by 24 by 30 inches is a start. They need hours out, a shower, and a diet of pellets and vegetables rather than a sunflower habit. A stressed or excited pionus can smell musky, and that scent fades when the bird settles. It is a breed quirk, not a dirty cage, and it is not a reason to ignore a bird that is fluffed or losing weight.</p>
<p>Talking is moderate. A pionus that never speaks is normal. A pionus that screams all evening may be bored, hormonal, or under-slept. Twelve hours of dark, quiet night fixes more parrots than another toy.</p>
<h2>The snuffle versus the sick bird</h2>
<p>Learn the excited snuffle so you do not rush a healthy bird to a clinic, and so you do not ignore a real infection because someone online said pionus always wheeze. Weight loss is the fact that settles the argument. <a href="https://www.aav.org">AAV</a> lists avian vets. A bird that stays fluffed belongs on the <a href="/vet/symptoms/bird-quiet">quiet bird page</a>.</p>`,
  },

  "breeds/birds/pacific-parrotlet.html": {
    description: "Pacific parrotlets are about 4.5 to 5 inches and often live 15 to 20 years. The size is finch-like. The bite and the attitude are not.",
    faqTitle: "Pacific parrotlet questions",
    faqs: [
      { q: "Are parrotlets good for children?", a: "No. They bite hard for their size and they bond to one person. A child who wants a pet to hold will be bitten, and the bird will be scared." },
      { q: "Can two parrotlets share a cage?", a: "Often they fight. One bird with a person is the safer plan unless you have a true bonded pair and a cage large enough to separate them the day it goes wrong." },
      { q: "Do they talk?", a: "A few words, sometimes. Buy one because you want a tiny parrot, not because you want a lecture." },
    ],
    html: `<h2>Five inches and a full parrot</h2>
<p>Pacific parrotlets, Forpus coelestis, are about 4.5 to 5 inches and an ounce, with a life often 15 to 20 years. They are bold, loud for the size, and affectionate on their terms. A cage that would satisfy a pair of finches is a jail. Give length to fly or at least to sprint, and hours on a play stand. Pellets, vegetables, and a little seed are the diet. Millet as the whole bowl is how a parrotlet gets fat and short of vitamins.</p>
<p>They nest in pockets and drawers if you let them. Hormonal biting follows a dark hut and a lot of petting on the back. Keep hands off the back. Keep the cage in the room where life is, not in a spare bedroom.</p>
<h2>A bite the size of the bird</h2>
<p>The beak bruises. Children should watch, not handle. A fluffed parrotlet crashes faster than a macaw because there is no body reserve. Weigh the bird. <a href="https://www.aav.org">AAV</a> is the vet search. The <a href="/vet/symptoms/bird-quiet">quiet bird page</a> is what a puffed-up, silent parrotlet looks like.</p>`,
  },

  "breeds/birds/lineolated-parakeet.html": {
    description: "Lineolated parakeets are about 6 to 7 inches and often live 10 to 20 years. They are one of the quieter parrots, and they still need a flight cage and a pellet diet.",
    faqTitle: "Lineolated parakeet questions",
    faqs: [
      { q: "Are linnies quiet enough for an apartment?", a: "They are quieter than a conure. They are not silent. A content linnie chirps. A linnie with nothing to do still finds a noise." },
      { q: "Do they like hands?", a: "Some become tame. Many prefer to be near you and not held. Forcing a skittish linnie makes a biter or a bird that freezes. Let the bird set the pace." },
      { q: "What cage do they need?", a: "Long enough to fly. A tall skinny cage wastes the bird. Width is the point, and a minimum around 24 by 24 by 36 inches is only a start if they never leave it." },
    ],
    html: `<h2>The quiet parakeet that still flies</h2>
<p>Lineolated parakeets, Bolborhynchus lineola, are about 6 to 7 inches. Lives of 10 to 20 years show up in careful homes. They are often called beginner birds because the noise is low and the diet is straightforward. Beginner does not mean a budgie cage and a seed stick. They need room to fly in short bursts, pellets, vegetables, and a little seed.</p>
<p>They can live as a pair if you accept that the pair will care more about each other than about your hand. A single bird needs your time. They are less likely to scream than a conure and just as likely to pluck if the cage is boring and the sleep is short.</p>
<h2>Sleep and a scale</h2>
<p>Twelve dark hours change the temperament of small parrots more than a new toy. Weigh a linnie weekly. A thin bird is a sick bird before the feathers look bad. <a href="https://www.aav.org">AAV</a> clinics see them. A bird on the floor of the cage is the <a href="/vet/symptoms/bird-quiet">quiet bird page</a>.</p>`,
  },

  "breeds/birds/society-finch.html": {
    description: "Society finches are about 4 to 5 inches and often live 5 to 8 years. They are domestic birds, not a wild species you can release, and they do not want to be held.",
    faqTitle: "Society finch questions",
    faqs: [
      { q: "Do society finches need a pair?", a: "They are social. One finch is a lonely finch. A small group in a flight cage is the point of the species. They are a poor choice if you wanted a single bird on your finger." },
      { q: "Can I hold them?", a: "No. Handling is stress. Watch them. A grab can kill a bird this light. If you want a hands-on pet, get a budgie or a cockatiel and learn that one." },
      { q: "What do they eat?", a: "A finch seed mix is not the whole diet. Add greens, egg food during a molt or breeding, and a cuttlebone. Obesity and itchy legs from a seed-only bowl are common." },
    ],
    html: `<h2>A pet that was never wild</h2>
<p>Society finches, Lonchura striata domestica, are about 4 to 5 inches and often live 5 to 8 years. They were shaped in captivity. There is no forest to return one to. They chirp quietly, they do not talk, and they do best in a group with room to fly. A cage of 24 by 12 by 18 inches is a crowded minimum for a pair and wrong for a flock.</p>
<p>They foster other finches' chicks in aviaries, which is why breeders keep them. In a pet home that habit is irrelevant. Give perches of different thicknesses, a bath, and no draft. Night frights happen. A small night light helps some rooms and is not a substitute for a cover and quiet.</p>
<h2>Feet, air, and a bird that hides illness</h2>
<p>Scaly leg mites, air-sac mites, and a bird that sleeps on two feet in a fluffy ball are the problems. Finches drop dead looking "suddenly" sick because they hid it. Weigh them if you can, or at least notice who is not eating at the dish. <a href="https://www.aav.org">AAV</a> lists clinics. The <a href="/vet/symptoms/bird-quiet">quiet bird page</a> is the emergency look.</p>`,
  },

  "breeds/birds/gouldian-finch.html": {
    description: "Gouldian finches are about 5 inches and often live 6 to 8 years. The colors are real. So are air-sac mites. They are an aviary finch, not a handling pet.",
    faqTitle: "Gouldian finch questions",
    faqs: [
      { q: "Are Gouldians good for beginners?", a: "Usually no. They are less forgiving of cold, drafts, and a seed-only diet than a society finch. Start with societies if you have never kept finches." },
      { q: "Can I hold a Gouldian?", a: "No. They stress, and a grab injures them. The color is for watching in a flight cage." },
      { q: "What are air-sac mites?", a: "Sternostoma mites that live in the airway. A finch that clicks when it breathes, or that wipes its beak and gasps, needs a vet. The treatment is a drug, not a different seed." },
    ],
    html: `<h2>Color that does not make them sturdy</h2>
<p>Gouldian finches, Erythrura gouldiae, are about 5 inches. Six to eight years is a reasonable life for a captive-bred bird kept warm and dry. Wild birds are an Australian conservation story. Pets should be captive-bred. They need a flight cage, not a canary show cage, and a group if you know how to watch for bullying. Males in breeding condition can harass each other.</p>
<p>Seed, greens, egg food, and a calcium source are the diet. A vitamin powder shaken onto seed and then knocked off is not a diet. They are native to warm country. A drafty window in January kills more Gouldians than a missed millet spray.</p>
<h2>The click in the breath</h2>
<p>Air-sac mites are the classic Gouldian illness. So are coccidia and a bird that is egg bound if you let them breed without a plan. Do not breed them because the colors would be pretty. <a href="https://www.aav.org">AAV</a> is the clinic list. A finch on the floor is the <a href="/vet/symptoms/bird-quiet">quiet bird page</a>.</p>`,
  },

  "breeds/birds/dove.html": {
    description: "Ring-necked doves are about 12 inches and often live 12 to 20 years. They coo, they are not parrots, and a pair will bond to each other more than to your hand.",
    faqTitle: "Ring-necked dove questions",
    faqs: [
      { q: "Do doves talk?", a: "No. They coo. The sound is softer than a parrot scream and it is still constant in a breeding pair. Know that before you put the cage by a desk." },
      { q: "Can I keep one dove?", a: "A single dove can bond to a person and still needs a flock-sized amount of attention. A pair is the more natural picture, and the pair may ignore you." },
      { q: "What do they eat?", a: "A dove or pigeon mix, grit, and greens. Parrot pellets as the whole diet are the wrong shape of plan. Seed alone, with no mineral, is also wrong." },
    ],
    html: `<h2>A coo, not a sentence</h2>
<p>Ring-necked doves, Streptopelia risoria, are about a foot long and often live 12 to 20 years. Care is beginner if you mean diet and cage cleaning, and intermediate if you mean you understand they are birds with dusty down and a hormone season. A cage of 24 inches cube is a minimum for a pair that also gets out, and it is small if they never do.</p>
<p>They are gentle and they startle. Children who chase them will have a dove that lives on the cage floor in fear. They are not a stepping-stone to a macaw. They are the pet, for a decade or two.</p>
<h2>Eggs, dust, and a bird that hides illness</h2>
<p>Females lay even without a male. Chronic laying drains calcium. A dove straining on the cage floor may be egg bound, which is an emergency. <a href="https://www.aav.org">AAV</a> clinics see doves. Fluffed and quiet is the <a href="/vet/symptoms/bird-quiet">quiet bird page</a>, same as any other bird.</p>`,
  },

  "breeds/birds/lorikeet.html": {
    description: "Rainbow lorikeets are about 10 to 12 inches and often live 20 to 30 years. The diet is nectar. The droppings are liquid, the noise is high, and this is an advanced bird.",
    faqTitle: "Rainbow lorikeet questions",
    faqs: [
      { q: "Can lorikeets eat seed?", a: "Seed is not their diet. They eat nectar, pollen, and fruit. A lorikeet on a cockatiel mix fails slowly. Use a commercial nectar made for them and clean the cups the same day, because nectar grows bacteria." },
      { q: "Why is the cage so messy?", a: "Their droppings are squirted and wet. Walls, cages, and your shirt get painted. People who hate that mess should not keep a lorikeet." },
      { q: "Are they cuddly?", a: "They can be, and they bite when they are hormonal or overstimulated. The noise is high. An apartment is a poor match." },
    ],
    html: `<h2>Nectar, noise, and a hose</h2>
<p>Rainbow lorikeets, Trichoglossus moluccanus, are about 10 to 12 inches and often live 20 to 30 years. Care is advanced because the food is wet, the mess is constant, and the bird is loud and busy. A cage of 24 by 24 by 36 inches is only a start. They need flight or a long time out, and a room you can wash.</p>
<p>They are not a quiet colorful conure. They scream. They play hard. A bored lorikeet plucks or bites. Captive-bred birds are the ones to buy. Wild-caught lorikeets are a conservation and a disease problem.</p>
<h2>Bacteria in the nectar cup</h2>
<p>Change nectar daily, wash the feeders, and do not leave fruit to ferment. A bird with a dirty vent or a sour crop is a husbandry failure headed for a vet. <a href="https://www.aav.org">AAV</a> is the search. A lorikeet that stops eating nectar and sits fluffed is the <a href="/vet/symptoms/bird-quiet">quiet bird page</a>.</p>`,
  },

  "breeds/birds/hyacinth-macaw.html": {
    description: "Hyacinth macaws are about 39 to 40 inches and can live 50 to 60 years or more. They are the largest macaw, a specialist diet of nuts, and an expert-only bird with a purchase price in the tens of thousands.",
    faqTitle: "Hyacinth macaw questions",
    faqs: [
      { q: "How big is the commitment?", a: "A bird that can outlive you, a custom enclosure, and a yearly food bill that can run to several thousand dollars in nuts and pellets. If that sentence feels abstract, you are not ready." },
      { q: "Are they gentle because they are expensive?", a: "Some are affectionate. The beak can crush. Price does not select for a safe parrot. Hormonal hyacinths bite the person they loved in March." },
      { q: "Are wild hyacinths pets?", a: "They are endangered. A legal pet is captive-bred, with paperwork. A bargain hyacinth is a smuggling story or a sick bird." },
    ],
    html: `<h2>The largest parrot is still a parrot</h2>
<p>Hyacinth macaws, Anodorhynchus hyacinthinus, are about 39 to 40 inches and 2.6 to 3.7 pounds. Fifty to sixty years is the working lifespan, and older birds exist. Noise is a deep shout that carries. Talking is moderate. Care is expert. The diet is built around nuts, especially hard palm nuts in the wild, and in a house it is a measured nut ration plus pellets, vegetables, and fruit. A bowl of walnuts as the whole menu is how a hyacinth gets fat and short of nutrients.</p>
<p>The enclosure is custom. A living-room cage sold for a blue-and-gold is a toy. They destroy furniture with the beak that opens macadamia nuts. Plan the room, the second caregiver, and the will.</p>
<h2>Paperwork and a clinic that will see a bird this size</h2>
<p>CITES paperwork should exist for a legal bird. Ask an avian vet, found through <a href="https://www.aav.org">AAV</a>, whether they can hospitalize a hyacinth before you need that answer at 9 p.m. A quiet, fluffed macaw is the <a href="/vet/symptoms/bird-quiet">quiet bird page</a> scaled up to an emergency you cannot lift alone.</p>`,
  },

  "breeds/birds/green-wing-macaw.html": {
    description: "Green-wing macaws are about 34 to 37 inches and often live 50 to 60 years. They are huge, loud, and sometimes calmer than a scarlet. Calm does not mean a beginner bird.",
    faqTitle: "Green-wing macaw questions",
    faqs: [
      { q: "Are green-wings quieter than scarlets?", a: "Some are steadier. The noise is still high. Do not buy one as the quiet macaw. The neighbors will correct that plan." },
      { q: "How big is the cage?", a: "A minimum around 40 by 50 by 65 inches is already a small room, and the bird still needs hours outside it. Measure the door the cage has to come through." },
      { q: "Do they talk?", a: "Many learn words. Many scream more than they talk. Speech is a bonus on a bird you can house for 50 years, not the reason to buy one." },
    ],
    html: `<h2>Red and green, and the size of a toddler</h2>
<p>Green-wing macaws, Ara chloropterus, are about 34 to 37 inches and 2.5 to 3.5 pounds. Lifespan is often 50 to 60 years or more. They have a reputation for being the calmer large macaw. Reputation is not a puppy class. They bond hard, they bite hard, and they need a person who can read a hormonal season without turning it into a wrestling match.</p>
<p>Pellets, vegetables, fruit, and a measured handful of nuts are the diet. A seed-only macaw is a vitamin deficiency with feathers. Chew toys are lumber, not a plastic supermarket bird toy. Captive-bred only.</p>
<h2>Fifty years of noise</h2>
<p>Have two people who can handle the bird, a will, and an avian vet from <a href="https://www.aav.org">AAV</a> who has seen a macaw. Heavy-metal poisoning from a cheap toy is a real emergency. A fluffed green-wing that will not eat is the <a href="/vet/symptoms/bird-quiet">quiet bird page</a>.</p>`,
  },

  "breeds/birds/meyer-parrot.html": {
    description: "Meyer's parrots are about 8 to 9 inches and often live 25 to 35 years. They are the quieter Poicephalus, related to the Senegal, and they are still a decades-long parrot.",
    faqTitle: "Meyer's parrot questions",
    faqs: [
      { q: "Are Meyer's parrots quiet?", a: "Quieter than an Amazon or a conure. They still shriek at dawn and when you leave. Apartment living can work and can also fail if the bird is bored." },
      { q: "Do they talk?", a: "Some learn a handful of words. Buy the bird for the temperament, not for a talking show. A silent Meyer's is a normal Meyer's." },
      { q: "How are they different from a Senegal?", a: "Meyer's are a bit smaller and often described as steadier. Both are Poicephalus. Both live for decades. Read the Senegal page if that is the bird in the photo, because shops mix the names." },
    ],
    html: `<h2>A small African parrot with a long lease</h2>
<p>Meyer's parrots, Poicephalus meyeri, are about 8 to 9 inches. Twenty-five to thirty-five years is a normal life. Noise is low to moderate for a parrot, which still means a morning call. Care is intermediate. A cage of at least 24 by 24 by 36 inches is a start, and the bird needs to be out of it. Pellets, vegetables, and a few nuts are the diet. Seed as the staple is the old mistake.</p>
<p>They can be one-person birds. They bite when they are hormonal or when hands become a game. They do not need a second parrot to be happy if a person is actually present. A second parrot of another species is often a fight.</p>
<h2>Captive-bred, and a scale</h2>
<p>Wild-caught Poicephalus are a poor buy. Ask where the bird was bred. Weigh a Meyer's. They are small enough that a week of "eating a little" is a sick bird. <a href="https://www.aav.org">AAV</a> is the vet list. Fluffed and silent is the <a href="/vet/symptoms/bird-quiet">quiet bird page</a>.</p>`,
  },
};
